      "<strong>v1.35.4 &mdash; Square credential whitespace defense + token-exchange error surfacing. Grasshopper&rsquo;s catch under v1.35.3 testing: CF&rsquo;s Variables and Secrets input field wraps long values visually, and that wrap can introduce embedded whitespace (newlines/tabs/spaces) into the stored secret value &mdash; not just leading/trailing, but mid-string. He&rsquo;d already checked leading whitespace and re-pasted both SQUARE_SANDBOX_APP_ID and SQUARE_SANDBOX_APP_SECRET, but token exchange still failed with the generic <code>?scheduler_error=token_exchange</code> redirect that hid Square&rsquo;s actual rejection reason.</strong> <strong>Two fixes in this ship</strong>: (1) <strong>Defensive whitespace strip on credential env vars at runtime</strong> &mdash; <code>process.env.SQUARE_*_APP_ID</code> + <code>process.env.SQUARE_*_APP_SECRET</code> now get <code>.replace(/\\s+/g, &quot;&quot;)</code> applied before use. Square credentials are alphanumeric + hyphens with no legitimate whitespace, so stripping all whitespace is safe and defends against the multi-line copy-paste artifacts CF&rsquo;s input field is leaking. Applied at every call site: <code>initiateSquareOAuth</code> (oauth-start), <code>oauth-callback</code> token exchange. (2) <strong>Bubble Square&rsquo;s actual error detail into the redirect URL</strong> &mdash; the prior <code>errReturn(&quot;token_exchange&quot;)</code> threw away Square&rsquo;s response message and emitted a one-word generic code. Now sanitizes Square&rsquo;s actual error string (limit 180 chars, URL-safe filter) and appends it as <code>scheduler_error=token_exchange__&lt;sanitized_message&gt;</code>. Visible in the browser Network panel response Location header on next attempt without needing wrangler tail. <strong>Diagnostic logging strengthened</strong>: both oauth-start and oauth-callback now <code>console.log</code> the RAW env-var contents alongside the resolved value AND the resolved credential string LENGTHS &mdash; so if the issue is still whitespace, the lengths will mismatch between attempts and reveal the contamination directly. <strong>What this doesn&rsquo;t change</strong>: the underlying CF input field bug is unfixable on our side; the strip + length-log is the workaround. The wrangler.jsonc <code>vars</code> block doesn&rsquo;t help here because secrets (not plaintext) can&rsquo;t live in wrangler.jsonc &mdash; they MUST be in CF&rsquo;s secret store. The strip-on-read pattern is the only durable defense until/unless CF fixes their input field. <strong>Touched</strong>: <code>src/routes/api.integrations.square.oauth-callback.ts</code> (strip + length-log + error-surfacing on token_exchange catch), <code>src/server/emma-scheduler.functions.ts</code> (strip + length-log in initiateSquareOAuth), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No schema change; no new dependencies; no Worker secret changes; no wrangler.jsonc change.",
