      "<strong>v1.35.2 &mdash; Square env routing hardening + diagnostic logging. Second live sandbox test attempt under v1.35.1 surfaced a NEW catch BEFORE Grasshopper clicked Allow: the Square OAuth consent screen rendered against PRODUCTION host (<code>app.squareup.com</code>) instead of sandbox (<code>app.squareupsandbox.com</code>), with the PRODUCTION client_id and the OLD 7-scope set, against his REAL Rejuv Skin Spa production Square account.</strong> Had he clicked Allow, Refill would have been authorized against the real production calendar, granting real appointment-write access for a sandbox debug. <strong>Root cause</strong>: <code>process.env.SQUARE_ENV</code> was being read at runtime as something OTHER than the literal string <code>&quot;sandbox&quot;</code> — almost certainly the same whitespace-contamination pattern Cloudflare&rsquo;s secret input field demonstrated on the NAME side of the secrets earlier in the v1.35.0 deploy chain (delete + retype was the workaround). This time the contamination was on the VALUE side of the SQUARE_ENV plaintext var. My code did strict equality (<code>process.env.SQUARE_ENV === &quot;sandbox&quot;</code>) at 7 separate call sites; any whitespace silently routed to production via the default branch. <strong>Fix</strong>: extracted a single source of truth <code>resolveSquareEnv()</code> helper in <code>src/lib/schedulers/square.ts</code> that does <code>(process.env.SQUARE_ENV ?? &quot;&quot;).trim().toLowerCase() === &quot;sandbox&quot;</code> &mdash; defensive against leading/trailing whitespace AND case variation. All 7 prior call sites (initiateSquareOAuth, disconnect dispatch, resync dispatch, OAuth callback, webhook receiver, rescue claim writeback, withFreshSquareToken) now route through the single helper. <strong>Diagnostic logging added</strong>: oauth-start + oauth-callback both <code>console.log</code> the raw env-var contents alongside the resolved value, so Worker logs surface whether sandbox routing actually engages on next live test. <strong>Architectural learning banked</strong>: Cloudflare&rsquo;s secret/plaintext input field can silently contaminate stored values with whitespace; defensive trim is required on ANY runtime config string-equality check. Same pattern needs to land on every future scheduler env-var (Mindbody, Boulevard, etc.) and probably every cron-trigger env-var that does <code>process.env.X === &quot;something&quot;</code> across the codebase. <strong>Touched</strong>: <code>src/lib/schedulers/square.ts</code> (new <code>resolveSquareEnv()</code> export), <code>src/server/emma-scheduler.functions.ts</code> (4 call sites + import + diagnostic log), <code>src/routes/api.integrations.square.oauth-callback.ts</code> (1 call site + diagnostic log), <code>src/routes/api.webhooks.scheduler.square.ts</code> (1 call site), <code>src/server/emma-rescue.functions.ts</code> (1 call site in dynamic import block), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No schema change; no new dependencies; no Worker secret changes. <strong>What this does NOT fix yet</strong>: the Rejuv Square production-side grant already exists on Square&rsquo;s server even though Grasshopper clicked Deny on the consent screen tonight. Should the v1.35.1 first-attempt callback have hit production Square too? Possibly &mdash; need to check whether Refill silently authorized against PROD on the first attempt where the user clicked Allow. The connection_row from that flow has merchant_id <code>{TBD}</code> &mdash; if it begins with the production format vs sandbox, the answer is yes and a PROD-side revoke is also owed.",
