      "<strong>v1.26.12 &mdash; Solo-practitioner override on the &lsquo;with X&rsquo; rescue copy.</strong> Surfaced during the v1.26.0 + v1.26.3 provider-affinity verification: Karen runs Rejuv Skin Spa with three providers (Michelle, Lisa, Karen herself), but in Acuity Karen&rsquo;s calendar is named &lsquo;Rejuv Skin Spa&rsquo; rather than her first name. The v379.2 <code>providerClause</code> suppression treated <code>provider_name == spa_name</code> as &lsquo;calendar named after the practice, suppress&rsquo; &mdash; which is correct for multi-practitioner spas where the calendar aliases the practice, but for Karen&rsquo;s setup it stripped the warmth signal: customers saw &lsquo;Tox at 6:50pm&rsquo; instead of &lsquo;Tox with Karen at 6:50pm.&rsquo; Naming the wrong claim: the original rule baked in &lsquo;calendar-as-practice&rsquo; as the only interpretation when in fact the same condition can mean &lsquo;solo practitioner branding under the practice name.&rsquo; <strong>Fix</strong>: new spa-profile lookup_key <code>owner-display-name</code> (existing knowledge_nodes pattern, no migration). <code>providerClause</code> now takes <code>ownerDisplayName: string | null</code> as a 3rd arg: when <code>provider_name == spa_name</code> AND owner-display-name is set, renders <code>${prefix}${owner}</code>; when null, suppression holds as before. <code>resolveOwnerDisplayName</code> helper added next to <code>resolveSpaName</code>; rescue-send call site fetches once and threads through all 3 composers (<code>composeRescueSms</code>, <code>composeProxySms</code>, <code>composeProxyEmail</code>). <code>getRescueOfferPayload</code> picks up the value via a parallel knowledge_nodes query (same Promise.all gather) and adds <code>ownerDisplayName</code> to <code>RescueOfferPayload</code>. Claim page (<code>rescue.claim.$token.tsx</code>) replaces the binary <code>showProvider</code> with a derived <code>displayProviderName</code>: different-from-spa &rarr; provider as-is; same-as-spa + owner set &rarr; owner; same-as-spa + owner null &rarr; null/suppress. Both &lsquo;claimable&rsquo; and &lsquo;already_claimed_by_you&rsquo; render branches updated; brand-green span (<code>#056048</code>) and Row label both source <code>displayProviderName</code>. <strong>Effect across providers at Rejuv</strong>: Lisa &amp; Michelle&rsquo;s calendars don&rsquo;t match the spa name &rarr; existing path renders &lsquo;with Lisa&rsquo; / &lsquo;with Michelle&rsquo; unchanged. Karen&rsquo;s &lsquo;Rejuv Skin Spa&rsquo; calendar &rarr; new path renders &lsquo;with Karen&rsquo; once the SQL paste in chat sets <code>owner-display-name = &lsquo;Karen&rsquo;</code> in her tenant. Other multi-practitioner spas that don&rsquo;t set the override continue suppressing per v379.2. <strong>Why a per-tenant scalar, not a heuristic</strong>: any inference based on appointment counts or calendar names is fragile (single-provider spas might rename a calendar mid-onboarding, multi-provider spas might have Karen-style calendar conventions, etc.). Explicit tenant config inverts the question from &lsquo;guess the right behavior&rsquo; to &lsquo;the spa told us their convention.&rsquo; The data model already supports this surface (knowledge_nodes <code>spa-profile</code> context, alongside <code>spa-name</code>, <code>hours</code>, <code>address</code>, etc.). No settings UI yet &mdash; for now the value lands via SQL; admin/owner settings surface for editing this is a v1.27 candidate. <strong>Touched</strong>: <code>src/server/emma-rescue.functions.ts</code> (providerClause signature + 3 composer signatures &amp; bodies + RescueOfferPayload type + resolveOwnerDisplayName helper + getRescueOfferPayload parallel-query expansion + 4 call-site thread-throughs), <code>src/routes/rescue.claim.$token.tsx</code> (displayProviderName derivation + 3 JSX swap sites), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No migration. SQL paste in chat sets Karen&rsquo;s override.",
