      "<strong>v1.26.11 &mdash; Baseline-alert suppression on first-sweep.</strong> Karen&rsquo;s Patterns tab opened to <em>200 unread &lsquo;promoted to VIP / Regular&rsquo; alerts</em> all stamped May 28 6:25 PM &mdash; a flood, not signal. Root cause: the bulk-recompute path can&rsquo;t tell &lsquo;brand new patient, first-time tier assignment&rsquo; apart from &lsquo;known patient crossed a threshold.&rsquo; The v1.25.7 RPC at step 1 of <code>recomputeReliabilityForUser</code> does <code>INSERT &hellip; ON CONFLICT</code> to upsert reliability rows, with the column default <code>tier='trusted'</code>. Step 3 reads them back as <code>trusted</code>. Step 4&rsquo;s in-memory diff sees <code>trusted &rarr; vip</code> (or whatever computeTier returns) and queues an &lsquo;update.&rsquo; Step 6 hands that to <code>composeAlert</code>, which <em>does</em> short-circuit when <code>fromTier === null</code> (line ~162, &lsquo;Initial assignment (no prior tier) is not an alert&rsquo;) &mdash; but the bulk path can&rsquo;t surface a null fromTier because the column default already filled it in. composeAlert sees a real-looking promotion and emits the alert. Every first-time-seen patient on a tenant&rsquo;s first-ever sweep got a fake promotion alert. Karen&rsquo;s first sweep: 200 of them in one batch. <strong>Fix</strong>: new Step 0 in <code>recomputeReliabilityForUser</code> snapshots <code>patient_node_id</code>s that already have a reliability row BEFORE the RPC runs. Step 6&rsquo;s alert-emit loop now filters <code>updates</code> through that pre-snapshot &mdash; patients not in the set are first-time tier assignments and don&rsquo;t fire alerts. Real transitions (a known patient crossing a threshold from one sweep to the next) stay in. One-shot SQL to dismiss Karen&rsquo;s existing 200-alert flood pasted to Supabase as part of this ship (system marker on <code>dismissed_by</code>). <strong>Why this is the right shape, not a UX layer band-aid</strong>: a tenant&rsquo;s first sweep is structurally different from every subsequent sweep &mdash; the first sweep populates the baseline; every sweep after compares against the prior baseline and surfaces movement. Putting the suppression at the engine layer means Karen&rsquo;s Patterns tab going forward shows only genuine transitions (someone hit VIP <em>tonight</em>, someone dropped to in_recovery <em>this week</em>) &mdash; signal, not noise. The <code>transitions</code> count on the v1.26.10 freshness pill also now reads &lsquo;real transitions&rsquo; only (was: real transitions + baseline noise). <strong>Anderson canary check</strong> (raised in the v1.26.10 verification but never surfaced cleanly): paste the Anderson lookup SQL on the next chat message into Supabase to read his tier + counts directly. Don&rsquo;t scroll 200 alerts to find him &mdash; that was a bad ask. <strong>Touched</strong>: <code>src/server/emma-reliability.functions.ts</code> (Step 0 pre-RPC snapshot + step 6 filter), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No migration. One-shot SQL paste handles existing Karen-tenant baseline alerts.",
