      "<strong>v1.26.10 &mdash; Persistent last-sweep result on the A-list freshness card.</strong> v1.26.9 shipped the &lsquo;Recompute now&rsquo; button + a toast that briefly read <code>X patients &middot; N tier transitions</code>, but the surface itself only showed <code>Last computed: &lt;time&gt;</code> &mdash; once the toast faded or the page reloaded, the user had no record of <em>what the sweep actually did</em>. Worse, the v1.26.9 freshness query sourced <code>latestRecomputedAt</code> from <code>max(recomputed_at)</code> across <code>emma_reliability_status</code>, but step 5 of <code>recomputeReliabilityForUser</code> only writes <code>recomputed_at</code> for rows whose <em>tier</em> changed &mdash; so a sweep with zero transitions didn&rsquo;t move the timestamp at all, and the card stayed stuck on the last sweep that flipped someone. Confusing for &lsquo;did the cron run last night?&rsquo; <strong>New table</strong>: <code>emma_reliability_runs</code> (migration <code>20260621000000</code>) &mdash; one row per <code>recomputeReliabilityForUser</code> call. Columns: <code>id, user_id, completed_at, patients_recomputed, transitions, trigger ('cron'|'manual'), created_at</code>. RLS: self-read via <code>auth.uid()=user_id</code>, service-role bypass. Composite index on <code>(user_id, completed_at desc)</code> covers the &lsquo;most-recent-run&rsquo; query. <strong>Server changes</strong>: <code>recomputeReliabilityForUser</code> now takes a <code>trigger</code> arg (default <code>'manual'</code>), anchors a single <code>completedAt</code> ISO string used for both <code>recomputed_at</code> stamps on changed rows AND the new runs-log row, and inserts the runs-log row at the end of <em>both</em> code paths (the early-return <code>updates.length === 0</code> branch + the full sweep branch). Returns <code>completedAt</code> alongside <code>patientsRecomputed</code> + <code>transitions</code>. The runs-log insert is best-effort (<code>console.error</code> on failure) matching the existing pattern_alerts batch-insert posture &mdash; a failure here is observability data loss only, the sweep itself succeeded. <code>getReliabilityFreshness</code> now reads from the runs log: three parallel queries (count of reliability rows, most-recent <code>emma_reliability_runs</code> row, fallback <code>max(recomputed_at)</code> on <code>emma_reliability_status</code>). The runs-log timestamp wins; the <code>recomputed_at</code> fallback covers the gap between migration deploy and the first sweep that writes a runs row. Return type expands to include <code>lastRun: { completedAt, patientsRecomputed, transitions, trigger } | null</code>. <code>recomputeReliabilityNow</code> passes <code>trigger: 'manual'</code>; the cron route at <code>/api/cron/emma-reliability-sweep</code> passes <code>trigger: 'cron'</code>. <strong>UI change on the A-list rules page</strong>: the &lsquo;Reliability data&rsquo; section&rsquo;s single <code>Last computed: X &middot; N tracked</code> line becomes a stacked surface &mdash; <code>Last computed: &lt;time&gt; &middot; N tracked</code> followed by three pills: <code>X patients swept</code>, <code>Y tier transitions</code> (green when &gt; 0, neutral when 0 with explicit &lsquo;no tier changes&rsquo; label so success doesn&rsquo;t look broken), and a small <code>Manual</code> / <code>Nightly cron</code> chip telling the user which run they&rsquo;re seeing. Implemented as a new <code>FreshnessLastRun</code> subcomponent inside the route file. <code>handleRecompute</code> also stashes the freshly-returned run into <code>freshness.lastRun</code> so the surface updates immediately on click without waiting for the <code>loadAll()</code> reconcile. <strong>What Grasshopper should see on first deploy</strong>: the section loads showing the existing v1.26.9 <code>Last computed</code> line (from the <code>max(recomputed_at)</code> fallback) but no pills, because no runs row exists yet. First click of <code>Recompute now</code> writes the first runs row + populates the pills. The next morning&rsquo;s 02:00 UTC cron writes another row with <code>trigger='cron'</code> &mdash; if Grasshopper visits the page next day, the chip will read <code>Nightly cron</code>, confirming the cron is alive (which v1.26.9 was the first ship to make believable after the bulk refactor). <strong>Audit history side effect</strong>: with every sweep persisted, debugging &lsquo;why did Anderson&rsquo;s tier flip&rsquo; becomes a runs-log scan instead of an inference from <code>max(recomputed_at)</code>. Not exposed in UI today (no /admin/runs surface); the data is there for next-incident debugging. <strong>Touched</strong>: <code>supabase/migrations/20260621000000_v126_10_reliability_runs.sql</code> (new table + RLS + index + pgrst notify + verify SELECT), <code>src/integrations/supabase/types.ts</code> (table block), <code>src/server/emma-reliability.functions.ts</code> (recomputeReliabilityForUser trigger arg + runs-log insert helper + getReliabilityFreshness 3-query path + ReliabilityFreshness type expansion + recomputeReliabilityNow trigger='manual'), <code>src/routes/api.cron.emma-reliability-sweep.ts</code> (trigger='cron' on the per-spa call), <code>src/routes/app.refill.patients.a-list-rules.tsx</code> (handleRecompute writes lastRun into freshness + new FreshnessLastRun subcomponent replaces the inline line), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry).",
