      "<strong>v1.26.0 &mdash; Provider becomes a matching criterion in the rescue dispatcher.</strong> When a Michelle-booked appointment cancels, the open slot is fundamentally Michelle&rsquo;s &mdash; not Karen&rsquo;s, not anyone else&rsquo;s. The dispatcher should offer that slot to patients whose primary provider is Michelle, not the entire waitlist. Patient who normally sees Karen shouldn&rsquo;t get pinged about a last-minute Michelle opening &mdash; different provider, different relationship, low affinity, wasted send. Implementation lives in <code>selectFitPatients</code> at <code>src/server/emma-rescue.functions.ts:154</code>. After the existing treatment-type filter + patient hydration (phone / banned / opted-out checks), a new provider-affinity pass runs: one bulk query against <code>emma_appointments</code> across all remaining candidate <code>patient_node_id</code>s pulling <code>(patient_node_id, provider_name)</code>, then in-memory aggregation derives each candidate&rsquo;s primary provider as the most-frequent <code>provider_name</code> across their all-time appointment history (ties broken string-asc, stable enough for this scale). The fit pool is then hard-filtered: keep only candidates whose primary provider matches the open appointment&rsquo;s provider (case-insensitive trim, mirroring <code>providerClause</code> at line 245). <strong>Fallback policy</strong>: if the affinity filter produces ZERO candidates, the unfiltered pool is used &mdash; rescue value is too high to lose to an empty match. A structured <code>console.warn</code> with <code>event:rescue_provider_affinity_fallback</code> fires once per fallback so we can observe how often the broader pool kicks in. <strong>Gate at the call site</strong>: <code>providerForMatch</code> is computed in <code>dispatchRescueAttempt</code> as the appointment&rsquo;s provider name UNLESS it&rsquo;s null OR matches the spa name (the same Acuity-calendar-named-after-the-practice case that <code>providerClause</code> suppresses). Null short-circuits the affinity filter inside the matcher &mdash; no behavior change vs. today for spas without provider data or with spa-as-calendar setups. <strong>What does NOT change</strong>: message composition. The body of the rescue SMS / email already includes &ldquo;with Michelle&rdquo; via <code>providerClause</code> (line 245) when provider differs from spa name; that&rsquo;s the existing provider mention in the wire and it stays. No sender-identity override, no display-name refactor &mdash; provider-match is a matching engine criterion, not a sender persona. <strong>Honesty note</strong>: I initially misread &ldquo;another criteria is &lsquo;provider&rsquo;&rdquo; as a sender-identity ask and drafted a plan around <code>overrideFromDisplayName</code> + opener rewrites. Grasshopper corrected pre-implementation. Plan re-scoped to matching engine before any code was written. Touched: <code>src/server/emma-rescue.functions.ts</code> (selectFitPatients + dispatchRescueAttempt call site), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No migration, no UI, no schema.",
